{"concepts_used":["GC-0107","GC-0072","GC-0073","GC-0084","GC-0133"],"decline_reason":"","declined":false,"citations":[{"ref":"TMA1995/s169","quote":"A certification trade mark is a sign used, or intended to be used, to distinguish goods or services: (a) dealt with or provided in the course of trade; and (b) certified by a person (owner of the certification trade mark), or by another person approved by that person, in relation to quality, accuracy or some other characteristic, including (in the case of goods) origin, material or mode of manufacture; from other goods or services dealt with or provided in the course of trade but not so certified."},{"ref":"TMA1995/s169","quote":"Note: The goods or services certified may be those of any person, including the owner of the certification trade mark or any person approved by the owner for the purpose of certifying goods or services."},{"ref":"TMM/Part35/1#1","quote":"1.3 The owner of the certification trade mark (or a person approved by the registered owner) can use the trade mark on goods or services in respect of which it is registered (see the Note to section 169) provided that the use is in accordance with the rules governing the use of the certification trade mark (section 171)."},{"ref":"TMM/Part35/1#1","quote":"1.4 The rules governing use of a certification trade mark must be assessed and approved by the Australian Competition and Consumer Commission (Commission) before the trade mark can be accepted for registration."}],"answer":"**Applying in a business name:** The supplied passages do not answer whether a business name can be named as the applicant. If you mean registering the business name itself as the certification mark, they also do not resolve whether that name would qualify. I cannot confirm either point from these extracts.

**What the Act requires:** Section 169 defines a certification trade mark by its function: distinguishing certified goods or services from those not so certified, in relation to quality, accuracy or another characteristic. It also expressly allows the certified goods or services to be those of any person, including the owner or an approved certifier. [TMA1995/s169]

**Who can use it after registration — Manual practice:** The Manual says that the owner, or a person approved by the registered owner, can use the mark on the goods or services for which it is registered, provided the use complies with the rules governing the certification mark. It also says those rules must be assessed and approved by the ACCC before acceptance for registration. These are statements of Manual practice; the supplied legislation does not include section 171 itself. [TMM/Part35/1#1]"}
